\section{Checking the premise}{

With \code{premise = TRUE}, every request that answers the question carries the
rule: the single answering request of \code{stuff}, \code{retrieve}, \code{rerank},
\code{skim}, \code{hierarchical} and \code{preview}; each chunk's request and the merge of
\code{map_reduce}; the draft and every revision of \code{refine}; each step and the
final answer of \code{iterative}; and the adjudication of \code{ensemble}. Requests
that only pick, score, summarise or copy out passages do not.

\code{map_reduce} treats a chunk that contradicts the premise differently from
one that only lacks the answer. The second is dropped, as it always was.
The first is passed to the merge as a finding that says the premise is
contradicted and what the excerpt states instead, and the merge, told the
same rule, weighs it against the chunks that did answer and may itself
report the premise false. \code{notes$premise_chunks} lists the chunks that
reported it, whatever the merge decided. \code{ensemble} does the same with its
members (\code{notes$premise_members}). \code{refine} carries the verdict in its
draft, so a later excerpt can add to the correction or overturn it.
\code{iterative}'s step reports it in a \code{false_premise} field of its reply,
which is in the schema only when the premise is checked, and stops there.
A merge or adjudication that failed returns the findings joined, which is
no verdict: \verb{$premise} is \code{NA}, and the notes still name the chunks or
members that reported it.

The answer records the check: \verb{$premise} is \code{NA} when nothing contradicted
the question, and the correction when something did. \code{\link[=gr_compare]{gr_compare()}} and
\code{\link[=gr_read_many]{gr_read_many()}} add a \code{premise} column to their summaries, \code{print()} and
\code{\link[=gr_audit_report]{gr_audit_report()}} say when the model reported the premise false, and the
trace has a \code{read.premise} step with the outcome.
}

\section{Verifying quotations}{

A reader that puts the chunks it read into \verb{$evidence} has quoted them by
construction, so for \code{stuff}, \code{retrieve}, \code{rerank} and the default
\code{iterative} a passing check says only that the chunks were the ones sent.
The check means something where a model chose what to quote: \code{skim},
\code{extract}, \code{screen}, and \code{iterative} under \code{verify = TRUE}.

Two matchers score each quotation. The one that decides \code{verified} is the
check described in \code{\link[=gr_verify_evidence]{gr_verify_evidence()}}, unchanged. Beside it the answer
carries \code{quote_status}, \code{quote_sim}, \code{quote_coverage}, \code{quote_edits} and
\code{quote_critical_ok} from an edit-distance matcher that tolerates ligatures,
line-break hyphens and OCR letter errors while requiring numbers, units
and negations to be identical. It is recorded only, and decides nothing in
this release.

\code{verify = TRUE} changes what \code{iterative} sends (its schema and
instructions), so a cache or recording made without it does not answer a
run with it. The other readers send exactly what they sent before.
}

\section{Out-of-range values}{

Numeric arguments are clamped into a usable range and the change is warned
about, never applied silently: \code{top_k} [1, 1e4], \code{fan_in} [2, 32],
\code{max_levels} [1, 12], \code{max_rounds} [1, 20], \code{rerank_candidates} [1, 1e4],
\code{rerank_min_score} [0, 10], \code{preview_tokens} [100, 1e5],
\code{delay_between_calls} [0, 600],
token caps [16, 1e6]. The ranges of the settings added later are given
with each argument.
}

\section{Where the defaults come from}{

Each setting's default, its tier (\code{basic} for the few most users change,
\code{advanced} for the rest) and the evidence behind the value. \code{untested}
means no measurement backs the value; \code{cited} names the published source it
comes from. The settings added as declared parameters
(\code{accept_near_verbatim}, \code{max_merge_levels}, \code{restate_tokens}, \code{bm25_k1},
\code{bm25_b}, \code{lexical_blend}, and the reading options added after them) are
stored in the spec only when they are not their default, so a default spec
keeps the store and cache keys it had before they existed.
\itemize{
\item \code{on_overflow} (default \code{"warn"}, advanced): untested. Chosen so that a document too long for one request still gets an answer that says what was cut.
\item \code{context_order} (default \code{"relevance"}, advanced): untested. The order the readers used before the setting existed; the alternatives were added to be compared, and no comparison is recorded.
\item \code{restate} (default \code{"auto"}, advanced): untested. A setting so that gr_compare() can measure it on a corpus; no measurement is recorded.
\item \code{premise} (default \code{FALSE}, advanced): untested. Off, so a run sends the requests it sent before the check existed.
\item \code{verify} (default \code{FALSE}, basic): untested. Off, so a run sends the requests and accepts the answers it did before the check existed.
\item \code{effort} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. Unset: no effort is sent, and the provider's own default applies.
\item \code{reader} (default \code{"map_reduce"}, basic): untested. The reader that sends every chunk, chosen because it reads the whole document at any length; no comparison of readers is recorded.
\item \code{model} (default \code{NULL}, basic): untested. Not a tuned value: NULL takes the client's model.
\item \code{temperature} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. Not a tuned value: NULL takes gr_options("temperature"), which sends none.
\item \code{max_answer_tokens} (default \code{1500}, advanced): untested. Set as a generous cap for an answer; not measured.
\item \code{max_chunk_tokens} (default \code{700}, advanced): untested. Set as a cap for one chunk's finding; not measured.
\item \code{max_summary_tokens} (default \code{500}, advanced): untested. Set as a cap for one summary; not measured.
\item \code{top_k} (default \code{6}, basic): untested. A common starting value for retrieval; not tuned here.
\item \code{min_score} (default \code{-Inf}, advanced): untested. Not a tuned value: -Inf applies no floor.
\item \code{mmr} (default \code{1}, advanced): untested. 1 is plain top-k, the selection used before the setting existed.
\item \code{rerank_candidates} (default \code{20}, advanced): untested. Set when rerank was written; not tuned.
\item \code{rerank_min_score} (default \code{4}, advanced): untested. Set when rerank was written; not tuned.
\item \code{fan_in} (default \code{5}, advanced): untested. Set when hierarchical was written; not tuned.
\item \code{max_levels} (default \code{5}, advanced): untested. Set when hierarchical was written; not tuned.
\item \code{max_rounds} (default \code{4}, advanced): untested. Set when iterative was written; not tuned.
\item \code{preview_tokens} (default \code{1200}, advanced): untested. Set when preview was written; not tuned.
\item \code{members} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. NULL takes ensemble's own pair, retrieve and map_reduce; not compared with other pairs.
\item \code{cite} (default \code{FALSE}, basic): untested. Off, so the answer prompt is the one used before the setting existed.
\item \code{skim_model} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. Not a tuned value: NULL uses \code{model}.
\item \code{summary_model} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. Not a tuned value: NULL uses \code{model}.
\item \code{parallel} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. Not a tuned value: NULL follows gr_options("parallel"). It does not change output.
\item \code{delay_between_calls} (default \code{0}, advanced): untested. No pause. It does not change output.
\item \code{accept_near_verbatim} (default \code{FALSE}, advanced): untested. Off, as the code behaved before the setting existed. Not measured as a stopping rule; on the labelled quotation set, 25 of 46 quotations with one content word swapped score near_verbatim (NEWS, 0.6.0.9000).
\item \code{max_merge_levels} (default \code{6}, advanced): untested. The cap the merge code had as a constant; not tuned.
\item \code{restate_tokens} (default \code{1200}, advanced): untested. An arbitrary number, as its comment said when it was a constant; not measured.
\item \code{bm25_k1} (default \code{1.5}, advanced): cited. Robertson and Zaragoza (2009), "The Probabilistic Relevance Framework: BM25 and Beyond", give k1 between 1.2 and 2.0 as usual; not tuned here.
\item \code{bm25_b} (default \code{0.75}, advanced): cited. Robertson and Zaragoza (2009), "The Probabilistic Relevance Framework: BM25 and Beyond", give b = 0.75 as usual; not tuned here.
\item \code{lexical_blend} (default \code{0.5}, advanced): untested. An even blend, set when the offline path was written.
\item \code{unskip_k} (default \code{0}, advanced): untested. Off (0), so the plan is followed as it was before the setting existed. Not measured.
\item \code{reread_on} (default \code{"never"}, advanced): untested. "never", so a run makes the requests it made before the setting existed. Not measured.
\item \code{low_score} (default \code{"not_found"}, advanced): untested. "not_found", the behaviour before the setting existed: no answer request. Not measured.
\item \code{fusion} (default \code{"default"}, advanced): untested. "default" keeps the 0.6.0 ranking of each reader. Reciprocal rank fusion is not compared with it here.
\item \code{rrf_k} (default \code{60}, advanced): cited. Cormack, Clarke and Buettcher (2009), "Reciprocal Rank Fusion outperforms Condorcet and individual Rank Learning Methods", SIGIR, use k = 60; not tuned here.
\item \code{answer_format} (default \code{"parsed"}, advanced): untested. "parsed", the requests sent before the setting existed. Published comparisons of forced schemas and free text disagree; owner decision DEC-05 keeps the question open until a live comparison.
\item \code{premise_verdict} (default \code{"sentence"}, advanced): untested. "sentence", the requests premise = TRUE sent before the setting existed. Not measured.
\item \code{context_policy} (default \code{NULL}, advanced): untested. NULL: each reader cuts as it did before the setting existed, keeping the start of what it cuts. No policy has been compared with it.
\item \code{refine_state} (default \code{"draft"}, advanced): untested. "draft", the state refine carried before the setting existed. Not compared with "ledger"; gr_condensation_check() builds the test.
}
}

\section{Reasoning effort}{

The default models reason before they write a reply, and that reasoning is
billed as output and counted against the same output cap as the reply.
Nothing sets how much unless \code{effort} does, so a corpus's hundreds of
per-chunk calls reason as long as the one call that drafts its claims.

The effort is sent as \code{reasoning = {"effort": ...}} on the Responses API and
as \code{reasoning_effort} on Chat Completions, and a \code{\link[=gr_mock_client]{gr_mock_client()}} or
\code{\link[=gr_backend_client]{gr_backend_client()}} handler finds it in \code{params$effort}. It is sent only to
a model whose \code{\link[=gr_model_info]{gr_model_info()}} says \code{reasoning = TRUE}, and left out without
a warning for any other, which would refuse the field, as \code{temperature} is
left out for a model that refuses that; register a gateway's reasoning model
with \code{\link[=gr_register_model]{gr_register_model()}} to reach it. For the calls it covers it replaces
an effort the client's \code{extra_body} sets. A \code{\link[=gr_ellmer_client]{gr_ellmer_client()}} cannot take
it per call, says so once (\code{gr_effort_unsupported}) and does not record it
as sent. A value is passed
through as given; one that is not \code{"none"}, \code{"minimal"}, \code{"low"},
\code{"medium"}, \code{"high"} or \code{"xhigh"} is warned about (\code{gr_effort_unknown}) and
sent anyway, since providers add levels.

Each request's effort is in its trace step (\code{params$effort}, and an \code{effort}
column of \code{as.data.frame(trace)}), and \code{\link[=gr_audit_report]{gr_audit_report()}} shows it beside
what each stage cost. It is part of the \code{\link[=gr_cache]{gr_cache()}} key, and, when set, of
a recipe's store key in \code{\link[=gr_read_many]{gr_read_many()}}, of what \code{\link[=gr_compare]{gr_compare()}} counts as
a different recipe, and of the key a \code{\link[=gr_replay_client]{gr_replay_client()}} matches on, so
runs at different efforts never share answers. Unset, none of those keys
change.

The labels are the \code{stage} column of \code{as.data.frame(trace)}:
\code{segment.context}, \code{segment.proposition}; \code{stuff.answer}; \code{map.answer},
\code{reduce}; \code{refine.draft}, \code{refine.revise}, \code{refine.answer}; \code{skim.extract},
\code{skim.consolidate}, \code{skim.answer}; \code{retrieve.answer}; \code{rerank.score},
\code{rerank.answer}; \code{hier.summarise.L1} (and \code{L2}, ...), \code{hier.answer};
\code{iterative.step}, \code{iterative.final}; \code{ensemble.adjudicate}; \code{preview.plan},
\code{preview.skim}, \code{preview.answer}; \code{screen.decide}; \code{extract.chunk},
\code{extract.resolve}; and, for \code{\link[=gr_claims]{gr_claims()}}, \code{\link[=gr_outline]{gr_outline()}} and
\code{\link[=gr_synthesise]{gr_synthesise()}}, \code{claims.draw}, \code{claims.reconcile}, \code{outline.derive},
\code{synthesise.section}, \code{synthesise.batch}, \code{synthesise.merge} and
\code{synthesise.revise.structure} (\code{.cut}, \code{.register}). A merge that takes
more than one level labels the levels below its final merge \code{.level1},
\code{.level2} and so on, which its own name covers.

A starting point, not a default: low for the many mechanical calls and high
for the few that judge.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{effort = c("extract.chunk" = "low", "skim.extract" = "low",
           "rerank.score" = "low", segment = "low", "map.answer" = "low",
           "claims.draw" = "high", "outline.derive" = "high",
           "synthesise.section" = "high", "ensemble.adjudicate" = "high")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Reasoning shares the reply's cap, so a call at \code{"high"} can spend all of it
and come back cut off (\code{finish_reason = "length"}, warned about where it
matters). Give such stages room: \code{max_answer_tokens} here,
\code{max_section_tokens} in \code{\link[=gr_synthesise]{gr_synthesise()}}, \code{max_outline_tokens} in
\code{\link[=gr_outline]{gr_outline()}}.
Requests to a reasoning model are sent with an output cap of at least 2048
tokens whatever the effort, and the cost limits are checked at that cap.
}

\examples{
gr_read_spec("retrieve", top_k = 8)$top_k

# Out-of-range settings are corrected loudly, not quietly.
suppressWarnings(gr_read_spec("hierarchical", fan_in = 999)$fan_in)

# Reasoning effort by stage: little for the per-chunk calls, more for the
# merge. The trace says which request carried which.
ch <- gr_segment(gr_example(), list(method = "paragraph", max_tokens = 150))
cl <- gr_mock_client(function(m, p) "Revenue was 45.2 million dollars.")
sp <- gr_read_spec("map_reduce", model = "gpt-5.6-luna",
                   effort = c(map = "low", reduce = "high"))
tr <- gr_trace()
invisible(gr_read(ch, "What was revenue?", cl, sp, trace = tr))
unique(as.data.frame(tr)[, c("stage", "effort")])
# A loaded question, checked against the document. The mock plays a model
# that finds the excerpts contradict it.
cl <- gr_mock_client(function(m, p)
  "FALSE_PREMISE: Revenue rose 12\% in 2023, to 45.2 million.")
ch <- gr_segment(gr_example(), list(method = "sentence", max_tokens = 400))
ans <- gr_read(ch, "Why did revenue fall in 2023?", cl,
               gr_read_spec("stuff", premise = TRUE))
ans$premise
is_not_found(ans$answer)
}
